    "agent_last_message": "The tool is built and passes everything I ran against it. It builds with `cargo build --release --manifest-path output/Cargo.toml` and uses only the standard library, so `[dependencies]` is empty.\n\n## What it does\n\n`wordcount --input <file> --output <file>` writes the JSON report in the specified shape, following `docs/wordcount-spec.md`:\n\n- **Lines, characters and whitespace:** lines are counted by `\\n` bytes, characters are bytes, and words are split on exactly the six whitespace bytes. Rust's `split_ascii_whitespace` leaves out `\\v`, and `split_whitespace` also splits on Unicode spaces such as NBSP, so I avoided both.\n- **Exit codes:** 0 means the report was written, 1 means an invalid invocation, and 2 means an unexpected internal error. A panic and out-of-memory both exit 2; Rust's defaults would be 101 and a signal.\n\n## Judgment calls\n\nThe spec is silent on these:\n\n- **Invalid UTF-8 exits 1.** I read \"malformed input\" in the task as invalid UTF-8, since JSON can't carry arbitrary bytes. It is the main compatibility risk if you intended Latin-1 or binary files to be counted anyway.\n- **Lowercasing is full Unicode**, so `\u00c9cole` and `\u00e9cole` are one word, per \"the word in lowercase\".\n- **An unwritable `--output` exits 2**, because the task lists only argument and input problems under 1.\n- **`--help` is rejected with exit 1.** An independent review pointed out that exit 0 is defined as \"output written\". The error message is followed by the usage text.\n- **The report is written in place.** A write that fails halfway leaves a partial file and exits 2.\n\n## Speed\n\nI tested on a 4-CPU sandbox. A 1 GiB text file takes about 1.8 s on four cores, and a 200 MB file takes about 1.3 s on one core. The speed comes from three things:\n\n- The file is streamed, so memory depends on the number of distinct words, not file size.\n- A custom word table keeps short words inside their slot.\n- Files of 8 MiB or more are split among threads at whitespace.\n\nThe worst case is a file of almos",
